    id: 'll35-sovereign-and-small',
    stories: [{"kind":"parable","tone":"light","title":"Waiting for the Perfect Lathe","body":"Marcus wanted to be a woodworker more than he wanted breakfast. He subscribed to the magazines, bookmarked the tools, and could recite the specifications of a four-thousand-dollar cabinet saw the way other men recite baseball stats. There was only one thing Marcus had never actually done, in three years of wanting: make anything. \"I'm not going to start with junk,\" he explained to his brother-in-law Dev. \"When I do this, I'm doing it right. Top of the line, or not at all.\" Dev, meanwhile, had found a wobbly hand-me-down lathe at a yard sale for eleven dollars. It squeaked. One leg was shimmed with a folded matchbook. On it, badly at first, Dev turned a lopsided bowl. Then a slightly-less-lopsided bowl. Then, six months later, a set of candlesticks his wife actually put on the table. Marcus came for dinner and turned the candlesticks over in his hands, frowning. \"You made these? On that awful thing in the garage?\" \"On that awful thing,\" Dev agreed. \"Which is currently eleven dollars more of a woodshop than yours.\" Marcus opened his mouth to explain about the cabinet saw, and heard, for the first time, how it sounded. He went home and dug his grandfather's rusty chisels out of a drawer. His first cutting board was crooked and slightly burnt. His fourth was a birthday gift someone kept. By the time he could finally afford the good saw, he actually knew how to use it. Nobody who ever built anything despised the small first day. Only the people still waiting for the perfect one do that — and they build nothing at all.","verse":"Zechariah 4:10"},{"kind":"parable","tone":"solemn","title":"The Company Store","body":"In a mill town at the mouth of the valley, everyone bought from the company store, because the company paid in credit good only there. Elias had grown up in it: his wages went in one door and out the other, and each month he owed a little more than he made. He told himself it was simply how the world worked. When his daughter asked why they could never leave, he had no answer but a shrug. An old widow named Sella lived at the town's edge in a small, plain house that was entirely her own. She kept a garden, mended her own roof, owed no one, and — this was the strange part — she was always giving things away: eggs, bread, a warm coat, an evening's help. Elias asked her once how she managed to give so freely when she had so little. \"Because I owe nothing,\" she said. \"A man who owes the store belongs to the store. He cannot give what is already spoken for. But what is truly mine, I am free to hand to anyone.\" The words lodged in Elias like a splinter. He began small — a few rows of potatoes on a borrowed patch, then a goat, then a debt paid down instead of deepened. It took four hard years. But the morning he walked out of that store owing nothing, he understood that the ledger had never really been about money. It had been about who owned him. That winter, for the first time, Elias gave a coat away. The one who owes belongs to the one he owes. But a freed man is free — free at last to serve.","verse":"Proverbs 22:7"}],
