    "levels": {
      "child": "Jesus told a story about a farmer who threw good seeds everywhere. Some seeds fell on the hard path and birds ate them, some fell on rocks and dried up, some fell where thorns choked them, but some fell on good soft dirt and grew lots of food! The seed is Yahweh's Word, and your heart is the dirt. Ask the Holy Spirit to make your heart soft and good ground, so when you hear about Jesus you don't just smile — you go and DO what He says.",
      "teen": "Picture four friends sitting in the same service, hearing the exact same message — and a year later their lives look nothing alike. Jesus explained why with the Parable of the Sower: the seed is the Word, and the difference is the soil of the heart. One heart is a hard path where the enemy snatches the Word before it sinks in; one is shallow rock where faith feels great until real pressure hits and it withers; one is choked by thorns — the stress of life and the pull of money and stuff; and one is good ground that actually grows fruit. James nails it: be doers of the Word, not hearers only who deceive themselves. Which soil are you letting your heart be this week?",
      "senior": "The Parable of the Sower confronts a comfortable lie: that hearing the Word is the same as living it. Jesus is explicit — the same seed lands on four soils and yields four completely different outcomes, and He locates the difference entirely in the heart, not in the Word. The wayside heart never lets it penetrate and the wicked one carries it off; the stony heart receives with joy but has no root and collapses under tribulation; the thorny heart lets the cares of this world and the deceitfulness of riches strangle it into fruitlessness; only the good ground hears, understands, and bears fruit. This is why 2 Timothy calls us to study to show ourselves approved — a workman rightly dividing the Word — and why James calls the mere hearer self-deceived. The invitation, at any age, is to stop grading yourself by attendance and start measuring by fruit."
    },
