      {
        "kind": "parable",
        "tone": "light",
        "title": "The Gardener Who Pulled the Thorns",
        "body": "Naomi inherited a raised garden bed from the previous tenant, and the first spring she planted tomatoes with real hope. She watered faithfully, she checked on them at dawn, and for a few weeks the little plants climbed bravely. But she had left the bed as she found it — full of stubborn weeds she considered too small to bother with, and a creeping vine she rather liked the look of. By July the vine had swallowed everything. Her tomato plants were still alive, technically green, but starved of sun and pinched at the roots, and they set no fruit at all. Her neighbor Ruth leaned over the fence one evening and said gently, You know they're not choking themselves. Naomi laughed and admitted she'd been too busy to weed — too many other things growing that she'd let stay. So the next weekend she got down in the dirt and pulled. The pretty vine first, then the little weeds she'd excused, roots and all, until the bed was bare and honest and the tomatoes finally had light. It felt like loss at first, all that green in the wheelbarrow. But within a month the plants she'd nearly given up on were heavy with fruit, more than she could eat, enough to fill the neighbors' baskets too. The soil had been good the whole time. It had only been crowded. Sometimes the most spiritual thing a heart can do is decide what it will stop letting grow.",
        "verse": "Matthew 13:22"
      }
