    facilitator: {
      talkingPoints: [
        'OPEN ON THE STONE, NOT ON THE GRIEVANCE. If the room starts in its own hurt, the lesson becomes a venting session. Start with Isaiah 53:3, Psalm 118:22 and 1 Peter 2:4 — the two ledgers on the same stone, men’s and Yahweh’s. Establish first that being appraised and declined settled nothing about His worth, and only then let anyone bring their own story into the room. If the room is ready for it, walk the four words Scripture uses for that one act across its four citations of the psalm — refused, rejected, set at nought, disallowed — and let them notice that the sentence ends at the head of the corner every single time.',
        'THE SPOKEN PIECE IS THE OCCASION, NOT THE AUTHORITY. Say this out loud at the start. It is Darrell’s declaration, and it is treated as the door into a Word-first study — tested by Scripture, upheld where Scripture upholds it, corrected where the flesh runs past it. Do not let anyone quote the piece as though it settles a question; the texts settle the questions.',
        'THE HINGE IS MOVEMENT 6, AND IT IS THE ONE MOST ROOMS GET WRONG. Love is not a pros-and-cons list, but Scripture commands counting the cost (Luke 14:28) and commands choosing after weighing (Deuteronomy 30:19; Joshua 24:15). The line is TEMPORAL, not emotional: weigh fully before the covenant, never after it (Ecclesiastes 5:4-5; Matthew 5:37). A young person who does not learn this reads ordinary carefulness as disrespect and calls hesitation an insult.',
        'DO NOT SKIP MOVEMENT 11 FOR TIME. It is the movement the piece does not contain, and without it the lesson arms people. Three cautions: a guarded heart (Proverbs 4:23) is the same Bible as long-suffering love (1 Corinthians 13:4-7); absence is not a sentence we administer (Romans 12:18-19); and I am good can be a locked door with a peaceful face (Ephesians 4:31-32; Philippians 2:3). If the clock is short, cut the recap, not this.',
        'THE DEEPEST CORRECTION IS IDOLATRY-SHAPED, SO NAME IT GENTLY. A demand to be chosen with no comparison, made of a human being, puts a creature in the Creator’s seat (Jeremiah 17:5,7; Psalm 118:8). Most people in the room asking to be chosen are not proud — they are wounded, and they are asking a person for something only Yahweh can give. Say it as relief, not as rebuke.',
        'KEEP THE ADVERSARY AND THE FALSE GODS NAMED LOW. The 1 Kings 18:21 quotation carries baal in the lower case in our corpus; read it as it stands and do not elevate it in discussion (see L108).',
        'WATCH FOR THE TWO PEOPLE THIS LANDS HARDEST ON. One is the person who has been someone’s option for years and will hear permission to walk away — give them movement 11 before they act. The other is the person who is themselves the double-minded one in someone else’s life; James 4:8 is for them, and it is a mercy, not an exposure. Do not let the room identify either of them out loud.',
        'END ON THE ORDER IN REVELATION 17:14. "called, and chosen, and faithful." Faithfulness comes third — after the calling and the choosing, never as their price. Anyone who leaves believing they must perform to stay chosen has been taught the opposite of this lesson.',
      ],
      howToRun: 'Prayer + the anchor (5): read Psalm 118:22, Ephesians 1:4 and Song of Solomon 8:7 aloud (KJV). | Recap (10): L118 — the viral ninety-seven-percent number; where did you test a claim against the Word and the real research this week? | Read the occasion (5): summarise Darrell’s spoken declaration plainly — do not compare me, do not bench me, I am not the maybe, I was already chosen by peace, by purpose, and by Yahweh — and state that Scripture, not the piece, decides what is kept. | Teach the arc (25): the One who was not picked (Isaiah 53:3; Psalm 118:22; 1 Peter 2:4; John 1:29) -> Yahweh will not be an option (1 Kings 18:21; Joshua 24:15; Matthew 6:24; Revelation 3:15-16; Exodus 34:14) -> chosen before there was a list (Ephesians 1:4-5; 2 Timothy 1:9; John 15:16; Jeremiah 1:5; Psalm 139:14,16; 1 Peter 2:9; Deuteronomy 7:6-7) -> the scale of convenience (1 Samuel 16:7; 2 Corinthians 10:12; Galatians 6:4-5) -> count the cost, and WHEN (Luke 14:28; Deuteronomy 30:19; Ecclesiastes 5:4-5; Matthew 5:37) -> you do not analyze fire (Song of Solomon 8:6-7) -> the price was already paid (1 Peter 1:18-19; 1 Corinthians 6:19-20; 7:23; Matthew 13:46; Proverbs 31:10; Isaiah 43:1,4) -> not the maybe, the period (James 1:8; James 4:8; Ruth 1:16-17; Genesis 2:24; Ephesians 5:25) -> friend is not a word for convenience (Proverbs 17:17; 18:24; John 15:13; Proverbs 25:19; 2 Timothy 4:16-17; Psalm 27:10) -> the correction (Proverbs 4:23; 1 Corinthians 13:4-7; Romans 12:18-19; Ephesians 4:31-32; Philippians 2:3; Jeremiah 17:5,7; Psalm 118:8) -> already chosen, by peace, by purpose, and by Yahweh (Colossians 3:15; Romans 8:28; 2 Timothy 1:9; Isaiah 43:1; Hebrews 13:5; Romans 8:38-39; Revelation 17:14). | Do-it-now (20): each person names ONE ballot they have been standing on and says their worth out loud from Ephesians 1:4 and Isaiah 43:1; then sorts one relationship into BEFORE or AFTER the commitment and says what that means they owe it this week; then answers honestly whether any I am good of theirs is a peace or a locked door, and names the one step of kindness that would prove it. | Discussion (15): the prompts below. | Send-off (5): called, and chosen, and faithful — in that order; you are not waiting to be picked.',
      discussionPrompts: [
        'The builders whose whole trade was judging stones threw out the head of the corner (Psalm 118:22), and Peter records both verdicts at once — "disallowed indeed of men, but chosen of God, and precious," (1 Peter 2:4). Whose ledger have you actually been reading your worth out of this year? What would change tomorrow if you read the other one?',
        'Yahweh says He chose His people when they were "the fewest of all people:" (Deuteronomy 7:7) — He removed the metric on purpose. Where are you still trying to earn a place you were already given? Name the specific comparison you keep running.',
        'Movement 6 draws the line in time: weigh fully before a commitment, never after it (Luke 14:28; Ecclesiastes 5:5). Have you ever read someone’s careful thinking BEFORE a yes as disrespect? Or stayed in a comparison AFTER a yes and called it honesty? Which of the two is your pattern?',
        '"A double minded man is unstable in all his ways." (James 1:8) — all his ways, not just toward you. Does that change what you thought a maybe meant? And is there anyone toward whom YOU are the double-minded one, where James 4:8 is the word you need rather than the one you want to give?',
        'Song of Solomon 8:7 says everything a man owns, offered for love, "would utterly be contemned." Where have you tried to buy a place in someone’s life — with usefulness, availability, money, or being easy to keep around? What would it look like to simply stop paying?',
        'The lesson insists on movement 11: a guarded heart and a long-suffering heart are the same Bible (Proverbs 4:23; 1 Corinthians 13:7), and absence is not a sentence we pass (Romans 12:19). Is there a door you have closed as peace that is really a punishment? What is the one kind thing that would tell you the truth about your own heart?',
        '"Cursed be the man that trusteth in man, and maketh flesh his arm" (Jeremiah 17:5). Have you handed a person the job of settling your value — so that their yes became your god and their no became your verdict? Read Isaiah 43:1 and Revelation 17:14 aloud and say what changes about who you are waiting on.',
      ],
    },
