      howToRun: 'This lesson is long because it has two sources, and it is designed to be run in one sitting or split across two. If you split it: session one is movements one through five (the Giver, the window, short prayer, authority, covenant); session two is movements six through ten (the heart of stone, captive thoughts, the death of me, grace to glory, the destiny). Open by reading Romans 8:30 aloud and letting the room notice that every verb is His and that GLORIFIED is past tense. Do not start with the authority material — a room that meets exousia first will hear a technique, and this teaching only lands right when the Giver and the covenant come before it. Movement six is the pastoral centre; slow down there and give it real air, because someone in the room has a stone and knows exactly where it is. Two rules for the leader, non-negotiable. First, keep the fences attached every time you teach the authority: Sceva’s sons, according to His will, and Luke 10:20. Second, never let the window in movement two be preached in a way that makes a grieving person the cause of an unanswered prayer — bring Paul’s thorn into the same breath, every time. Close with the two-part practice in Take It With You, and give people quiet to actually pray the one sentence rather than promising to do it later.',
