    facilitator: {
      talkingPoints: [
        'Open by naming the restriction out loud: for this session the salvation plan comes from Genesis only, and the enemies from the first five books only. The restriction is the argument — if the plan is really Yahweh’s from the start, it should be legible at the start.',
        'Read Genesis 1:1, 1:2 and 1:3 as three consecutive lines and stop after each. Most rooms have never been shown the Holy Spirit in verse two. Let the silence sit there.',
        'Teach the Word as a Person by pointing at the mechanism: the speaking IS the doing. That is why Genesis 1 is the first place we watch the Son at work, not a later inference laid over the text.',
        'Do not rush Genesis 1:26. Read Us and Our slowly, then jump straight to Genesis 3:22 and read one of us. Two witnesses inside three chapters settles it without argument.',
        'Put the adversary’s three moves on a board — QUESTION, CONTRADICTION, PROMOTION — and ask the room to fill in a modern example under each. They will have one before you finish writing the third.',
        'Insist on the ORDER of Genesis 3: the search ("Where art thou?") comes before the sentence. A room full of people who expect Yahweh to lead with the verdict needs to see that He led with a question.',
        'Genesis 3:15 is the centre of the lesson. Read it as courtroom language spoken to the accused, and weigh the two injuries against each other — a crushed head against a bruised heel — until the room hears which one heals.',
        'Handle Genesis 3:21 tenderly. The first death is a covering Yahweh made and put on them with His own hands. Many in the room carry a picture of Yahweh as chiefly the one who extracts; this verse corrects it from the text.',
        'On Genesis 15:17, tell the room what walking between the pieces MEANT — the walker invoked the fate of the pieces on himself — and then point out that Abram was asleep. Let someone say out loud that Yahweh took both sides.',
        'Walk Genesis 22 in the order it happens: the only son whom he loves, the three days, the son carrying the wood, the question, the answer that turns out to be prophecy, and the ram in the stead of his son. Do not explain it. Read it and let it land.',
        'When you reach the enemy list across Exodus to Deuteronomy, keep it plain and unsensational. The Torah names them without dramatising them, and so should we — every one is a defeated creature, and Exodus 12:12 is a courtroom, not a spectacle.',
        'Close on the Three working as one, and answer the practical question it settles: no one in the room has to persuade one Person of the Godhead to agree with another. The Father sought, the Son was promised and came, the Spirit moved and moves.',
      ],
      discussionPrompts: [
        'Before tonight, where did you think the salvation plan began? What changes if it was published in Genesis 3, to the adversary, as his sentence?',
        'Read Genesis 1:2 aloud. Had you ever noticed the Holy Spirit in the second verse of the Bible? What does His being there before the light say about Him?',
        'Genesis 1:26 says Us and Our. Where else in these first chapters does Yahweh speak that way, and why does a second occurrence matter?',
        'Take the adversary’s three moves — a question about the Word, a contradiction of it, an offer of promotion. Which of the three do you personally fall for most often?',
        'Yahweh asked "Where art thou?" before He passed any sentence. What does that order tell you about how He comes to you when you have failed?',
        'Weigh the two injuries in Genesis 3:15 against each other. Why does it matter that one is a head and the other a heel?',
        'Genesis 3:21 — Yahweh made the covering and put it on them. What had they made for themselves first, and why was it not enough?',
        'Abram was asleep while Yahweh walked between the pieces alone. What fear does that lay to rest for you?',
        'Genesis 22:8 was spoken as an answer to a boy and turned out to be prophecy. Where have you spoken something in faith and only later seen what it meant?',
        'The Torah names our enemies plainly — the serpent, the gods of Egypt, devils, molech, baalpeor. Why is naming them soberly healthier than either ignoring them or obsessing over them?',
        'Genesis 50:20 says evil was intended and Yahweh meant it unto good, to save much people alive. Where is that sentence being worked out in your own history?',
        'The Father sought, the Son was promised, the Spirit moved — all in the first two chapters and the third. How does seeing the Three agree change the way you pray this week?',
      ],
    },
