      'Gives the timeline the Word itself carries, in the Word’s words: the split (1 Kings 12:20), Assyria taking the north (2 Kings 17:6, 17:18), Babylon taking Judah (2 Kings 25:21), the return under Cyrus (Ezra 1:1-5), the King born of Judah, and Rome’s siege foretold (Luke 21:20-24) — with the dates history has settled stated plainly (DR-0100).',
      'Settles where the tribes are from the Word, not from a theory: scattered and counted, never lost — "yet shall not the least grain fall upon the earth" (Amos 9:9); "to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad" (James 1:1); Anna of Asher still known in the temple (Luke 2:36); sealed by name at the end (Revelation 7:4).',
      'Names the lost-tribes debate in order to teach past it: the Word required a register before a lineage claim was honoured (Ezra 2:62), warns off "endless genealogies" (1 Timothy 1:4), and never names a modern nation as a tribe — the secret things are His (Deuteronomy 29:29); "The Lord knoweth them that are his." (2 Timothy 2:19)',
