        title: 'The Two Weights in the Same Bag',
        body: 'There was a grain merchant with a good name in the town, and he kept two stones in his bag for weighing. One was true. The other was shaved a little on the underside, which nobody could see unless they turned it over, and nobody turned it over, because he had a good name. When a man came to sell grain, the merchant weighed with the heavy stone, so the man’s harvest measured light and he was paid for less than he brought. When the same merchant sold, he weighed with the shaved one, so the buyer carried home less than he paid for. The merchant did not think of himself as a thief. He thought of himself as a man with a margin. And the town did not fail all at once. It failed the way a field fails when the rain is fine: the growers stopped bringing their best, because their best did not measure; the sons went to work for wages instead of planting, because planting did not pay; the market thinned, and the merchant’s own sons inherited a town with less grain in it than the one their father was given. On the day the scales were finally turned over, the merchant said what such men always say, which was that he had followed the custom of the market. And that was true. It was the custom. It was also the reason there was less bread on every table in town, including his.',
        verse: 'Proverbs 11:1',
