        title: 'The Seventh Year at the Door',
        body: 'The seventh year came for Eliab the way it came for everybody, which is to say on an ordinary morning with the smell of bread in it. His master found him in the yard and told him what the law required: that he was free, that he owed nothing, and that he was not to leave empty-handed. There was a young goat, grain out of the floor, and wine, because the law said furnish him liberally and his master was not a man who read around a word. Eliab stood holding more than he had arrived with and looked at the gate, which was open, and had been open since sunrise. He thought about the six years. He thought about the winter he had been sick and the broth that had come twice a day from the main house without anyone making a speech about it. He thought about his wife and the two children asleep inside. Then he said the sentence the law puts in a man’s own mouth, that he loved his master and his house and would not go out free. So they went to the doorway together, and the master took the awl — a leather-worker’s spike, the tool that punches a hole through a hide — and in front of witnesses he pierced Eliab’s ear against the very post of the door he had been free to walk through. Anyone passing afterwards could read the mark, and what the mark said was not that a man had been captured. It said that a man had been standing in an open doorway, holding a severance in both arms, and had turned around.',
