        tone: 'solemn',
        title: 'What the Price Was Paid In',
        body: 'Two ledgers survive from the same port, kept in the same decade, and a person can read them both in an afternoon. The first is a merchant’s. It records human beings by the line, with a figure beside each one in pounds and shillings, and the arithmetic is careful, because the arithmetic was the point. The men who kept that ledger went home at night to their own beds, and not one of them ever once considered writing his own name in the column where the payment went. That is not a moral failing peculiar to them; it is simply what buying a person is. The price is always paid out of something the buyer can spare. The second ledger is not really a ledger at all, and it has one entry. It says that the people in it were not bought with corruptible things, not with silver and not with gold, but with blood — and the name in the payment column is the name of the One doing the buying. Set the two documents side by side and the objection dissolves without anyone having to raise a voice. Both describe a purchase. In the first, the buyer spends money and keeps himself. In the second, the buyer spends himself and keeps nothing back. Whatever word you want to use for the second transaction, it is not the same transaction as the first, and a man who has read only the first page of history can be forgiven for thinking it is — right up until somebody shows him the second.',
        verse: '1 Peter 1:18',
