        body: 'Two houses on the same street lost everything in the same year. The first went in a night. A pan caught, the alarm screamed, the neighbours came running with hoses and blankets, and by morning there were casseroles on the porch and a fund at the church. Everyone agreed it was a tragedy. The second took nine years. A joint behind the laundry wall wept about a spoonful a day. No alarm is built for a spoonful. The family smelled something in the spring and called it spring. They painted over a stain and called it an old house. A man who fixed pipes told them plainly, in the first year, exactly which joint it was and what it would cost, and they thought his number was high and his manner was rude, so they did not call him back. Each year the floor gave a little more and each year they adjusted how they walked. By the ninth year the joists were gone and the house was condemned. Nobody brought a casserole. There was no fund. The neighbours said it was a shame, and one of them said the family should have known. But there had been no alarm, only a man with a number, once, nine years back. The first house was destroyed by fire. The second was destroyed for lack of knowledge, which is the same as saying it was destroyed by a correction that was offered and declined. And only one of the two got mourned, which is why the second kind keeps happening.',
